**Read:** [Fundamentals of Vacuum Technology — Walter Umrath and contributors](../sources/Fundamentals-of-Vacuum-Technology-Leybold.lingua#r_1S93A5X32978GSHZH4CF96VPQR), section 2.1.2.2.4 and the wet-process discussion in 2.2.3.

A condensable constituent can reach its saturation partial pressure while the trapped gas is compressed. Liquid formation changes the pump's inventory and can contaminate a sealing fluid. The relevant comparison is vapor partial pressure against saturation pressure at the local temperature, not total inlet pressure alone.

Gas ballast admits another gas during a suitable part of the compression cycle. It can allow the exhaust valve to open before the vapor reaches a condensing state. This changes total transferred amount, attainable pressure, exhaust composition and heat. Its effectiveness depends on timing, quantity, temperature and the chosen vapor.

**Work:** Use a hypothetical ideal-mixture compression path to follow vapor partial pressure with and without added noncondensable gas. Identify where a specified saturation relation predicts condensation. Close the amount balance and explain why ballast does not remove liquid already stored elsewhere or establish compatibility of a reactive gas mixture.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
